#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065358 :tutorial/content "Whenever we add a negative number, we write parentheses around the negative number so that it's easier to read. \n\nFor example, instead of\n\n$$\n7 + - 5\n$$\n\nwhich is difficult to read, we write\n\n$$\n7+(-5).\n$$\n\nAdding a negative number is the same as subtracting a positive number. Therefore, we can drop the addition sign and the parentheses in the above expression, which gives\n\n$$\n7 - 5.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the subtraction, as usual, we get \n\n$$\n7 - 5 = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7bae60ac-f313-592d-89f9-3c81ca1f8ffd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065359 :tutorial/content "We can add a negative fraction using the same method. To demonstrate, let's find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{7} + \\left(- \\dfrac{5}{7}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we eliminate the parentheses and obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 {7} + \\left(-\\dfrac 5 {7}\\right) = \\dfrac 2 {7} -\\dfrac 5 {7}.\n$$\n\nNow, since both fractions have a common denominator of $7,$ we can combine the numerators, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}-\\frac{5}{7} & =\\frac{2-5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $2 - 5$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/c6/c63a3db44b79e751c7cf1a49dcebcbb1d00cea0427da4ef271d07646e4be2748.png)\n\nWe find that $2-5=-3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2 - 5} {7} = \\dfrac{-3}{7} = -\\dfrac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nIf two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator first and *then* subtract! Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3edabc0b-b8c7-5264-aa38-ff64670a659b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3030 :tutorial/title "Adding a Negative Fraction"}]}
